          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"“Rich” chiefly signifies worldly self-sufficiency and misplaced reliance on possessions, often masking spiritual poverty; in a positive inversion, true richness consists in faith, grace, good works, or treasure toward God.\",\"book_summary\":\"“Rich” negatively identifies those who consider themselves self-sufficient and unreceptive to God, while true wealth consists in riches toward God, tested faith, and righteous covering.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Self-sufficient reliance on worldly wealth that conceals spiritual poverty, contrasted with genuine spiritual wealth in God.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Self-sufficiency that claims to need nothing and therefore remains spiritually poor, contrasted with true Godward wealth.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book states the negative sense more specifically as an inward posture of perceived needlessness rather than material possession by itself. This is already explicit in the consensus, which distinguishes dangerous reliance on wealth from literal wealth and recognizes true spiritual riches.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book’s negative and positive senses correspond directly to the consensus’s dominant reading and recognized spiritual-wealth alternative.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to their minimum cores, both readings identify false richness with self-sufficient spiritual complacency and true richness with wealth toward God. The book’s language about inability to receive, tested faith, and righteous covering adds interpretive detail without replacing the referent or materially changing the covered cases.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The phrases “cannot receive,” “tested faith,” and especially “righteous covering” are interpretive expansions rather than fully explicit definitions in the cited verses; nevertheless, Revelation 3:17–18 and Luke 12:21 strongly establish the central contrast.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
